1399be7f/workbench/query.sql"], ["Bash", "git log --oneline -10 && ls && grep -rIl -iE \"create (unique )?index|create table\" . --exclude-dir=.git --exclude-dir=node_modules | head"]], "final_message": "The likely culprit is `lower(u.email)` in the WHERE clause. I only have the query. There's no schema or `EXPLAIN` output in the repo, so this is a diagnosis from the query text, not something I've measured.\n\n**Why it's slow**\n- Wrapping the column in `lower()` stops the database from using a plain index on `users.email`. It has to scan every row in `users`, lowercase each email, and compare.\n- Any full scan gets slower as `users` grows. That could explain why it got bad \"since last week\" without anyone touching the query. It's also possible someone recently added the `lower()` or dropped an index, so check `git log` or `git blame` on wherever the app builds this query.\n\n**Fixes, in order of preference**\n1. **Add an expression index** that matches the query. In Postgres:\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n   ```\n   The query then uses it without any change. In MySQL 8 you'd use a functional index, and in SQL Server a computed column with an index.\n2. **Normalize emails on write** (store them lowercase, or use `citext` in Postgres). Then query with `u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a plain unique index. This is cleaner long-term, but it needs a backfill.\n3. **Check that `orders.user_id` is indexed.** The join needs it. If it isn't, that could be the other half of the eight seconds, because each matching user would trigger a scan of `orders`.\n\n**Smaller issues**\n- `SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables, including duplicate `id` and `user_id` columns. Select only what the page renders.\n- If a user can have thousands of orders, add `ORDER BY ... LIMIT` and paginate.\n\nTo confirm, run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the query. A `Seq Scan on users` with a `Filter: (lower(email) = ...)` line confirms the diagnosis. If you see an index scan on `users` instead, the problem is elsewhere, probably the `orders` side. If you paste the plan, or the `\\d users` and `\\d orders` output, I can tell you which it is.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}